WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.730
- This is something that just
so many people get wrong.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:07.360
If you follow this advice
it will significantly impact

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.060
the quality and professionality

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.320
of your PowerPoint presentations.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.760
Hi guys, I'm Heinrich from Firm Learning.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.890
Welcome to another episode in my channel.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.870
This video is an excerpt
of my online course

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.550
on creating PowerPoint slide presentations

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.750
in the same way as top
management consulting firms

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.200
such as BCG, or Bain do.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.780
As you know I worked myself
in one of those firms

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.310
for over six years,

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.310
I created thousands of PowerPoint slides.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.440
This is really what my work was all about

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.120
for the majority of the time.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.490
And I gained very valuable
knowledge and insights

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.630
into how to create these presentations.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.400
In most professional jobs,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.682
one of the main reasons to use PowerPoint

00:00:46.682 --> 00:00:51.682
is to visualize data and to
present data in one chart.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.710
However, how exactly the data
is presented in the chart,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:59.560
and what the chart consists
of is very unclear,

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.170
and you see very elemental
mistakes being done here.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.590
When you create
presentations in consulting,

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.240
well actually any business at
all, there should be one rule.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.720
And this is that you should
be able to take out one page

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.310
of your presentation deck,
and that the page itself

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.790
should be self-explanatory to everybody

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.890
who just picks it up and reads it.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.740
And the reason is that very often

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.080
these kinds of presentations are not used

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.820
for Steve Job's style keynote
speeches or addresses.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.370
It's not really meant to be presented,

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.620
even though of course
sometimes this happens as well.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.900
But very frequently
these kinds of documents

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.420
are really used as documents,

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.570
that then are shared in the organization,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.870
sometimes saved for many
months and years to come.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.900
And then it is important
that maybe in a year from now

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.810
when a new colleague
enters the organization,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.370
picks out a presentation, reads something,

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:58.650
is able to understand what
was meant at the time.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.950
Especially because the employee
who may be created the deck,

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.740
or created a specific page,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.750
maybe he's not in the
company anymore at all.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.942
So this is why it's important
that a page speaks for itself,

00:02:10.942 --> 00:02:13.950
is self-explanatory, and nobody needs

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.820
to be there to really
explain it to somebody,

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.390
but you can just understand
it by looking at it.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.878
And this is exactly what
this video will focus on.

00:02:21.878 --> 00:02:25.430
We will talk about how to
visualize data in a diagram,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.390
and then specifically how the
diagram should be composed

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.040
in order for the diagram
to be self-explanatory.

