00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.979
Another really crucial concept
when visualizing your data

00:02:58.979 --> 00:03:03.979
is the concept of making your
charts speak for themselves.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:07.010
And what that means is the following:

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:10.100
You really have to think back
about how these presentations,

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.160
how these top management presentations

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.670
will be going to used.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.180
And the fact is that these presentations

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.750
often get sent around
among top management,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.880
or among other apartments,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.290
and then people have your
document in their hand.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.120
And they want to understand
what they see, by themselves,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.750
without you needing to stand next to them

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.880
and to take their hand and explain

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.290
every little thing to them.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.350
So what's really important

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.640
is that you are able
to create your charts,

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.990
and I think this is
especially relevant to charts

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.960
because charts can often be
confusing to many people.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.670
So it's really important that you are able

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.380
to create your charts in a way

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.390
that they are self-contained,
speak for themselves,

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.350
and contain all the information necessary

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.900
that are required for a
good comprehension of them.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.000
So let's just look into some things

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.150
which are really crucial
for that to be possible.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.096
So what are the things
that should be included

00:04:02.096 --> 00:04:03.790
in every chart?

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.340
The first thing is something like

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.580
clear description of a chart.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.280
So basically what do I see?

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.630
And here's just one example.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.410
So here is a chart
comparing different regions

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.250
on a certain kind of metrics.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:18.700
So the title could be here a bit generic,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.490
Market Dynamics per Region.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.510
And of course try to do it as tangible

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.630
and as concrete as possible.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:28.100
Then what's always important
is the unit of measurement.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.990
So what is actually shown?

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.670
And you would be surprised how
many charts exist out there,

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.610
presentations that do not even tell you

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.800
what the unit of measurement is.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.100
And this chart is a bit complicated

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.660
because it has three dimensions.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.620
You have this one axis,
you have this one axis,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.730
and then the bubble itself,
the diameter of the bubble,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:50.770
is the dimension for itself.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:52.060
And here of course it's crucial

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.080
that you give the unit of measurement

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.630
of every single dimension that you use.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.030
So here it says percentage of revenue,

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.860
here this is the percentage
of revenue as well,

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.033
and here the unit of
measurement is Euro million.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:08.730
And what I just want to highlight is

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.980
whenever you use percentage,
it's always important

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:16.603
to always say the percentage
of what is given here.

00:05:16.603 --> 00:05:18.150
And as you can easily see,

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.770
if you just talk about operating margin,

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:21.603
so it's more or less clear

00:05:21.603 --> 00:05:23.777
that this is some kind of percentage,

00:05:23.777 --> 00:05:27.750
but margins are defined
in very different ways.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.110
And it's not always given,
it's not always clear

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:33.140
that a margin is calculated
as a percentage of revenue.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.920
So really make it clear here

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.140
that you are referring to revenue.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.400
And the same is of
course true for the CAGR,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.350
for the compounded annual growth rate.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.780
So of course that as a percentage,

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.550
but the growth rate of
what are we looking at?

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.590
Of the profits, of the revenues,

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:51.140
or what are we talking about?

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:52.980
So always really make clear,

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.960
just make it a good habit of yours

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.210
that whenever you write percentage,

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.300
that you write percentage of something,

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.150
and make clear what the something is.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:05.850
Another thing that's really important

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.870
but very often forgotten is the time.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:12.820
So from when is this data coming from?

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:14.450
What data do I see?

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.150
And here it's given the year, 2014.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:18.720
Here is given the timeframe

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.880
of the growth rate we're looking
at, so here only two years.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:23.540
And this is really important

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:27.100
because it's not really
intuitive what that would be.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.090
Because often these presentations
might be sent around

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.860
one or two years later
after you created them,

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:37.140
and then nobody knows anymore
from when the data is.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.090
But even if you create it right now,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.580
and you are basing the data on
some kind of research report,

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:44.250
for instance, that you found somewhere.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.810
In this case it's some
kind of annual report,

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:48.572
so it's not a research report,

00:06:48.572 --> 00:06:51.460
but the data is coming from somewhere,

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:53.170
from some kind of source.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:56.460
And the source is probably
not basically created today,

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:57.830
but in the past.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.850
Sure that you indicate
clearly from when the data is,

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:02.750
so that the audience, or your readers,

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:04.073
they really can know that.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:09.220
Another important thing is
the legend, or chart key.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.930
So whenever you use some
kind of color coding,

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.837
or other things that are
necessary to understand,

00:07:14.837 --> 00:07:16.030
use a legend.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.420
And here we're using a
legend for the circles,

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:22.270
to explain that the diameter
of the circles matter,

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.310
that they mean something,

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.660
diameter based on market size.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:27.590
And then what you see here

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.850
is that these two circles are colored,

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.230
so these are blue, whereas
the others are gray.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:34.240
So here has not included
a legend for that,

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.710
you could surely do that.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.410
And the reason why here
has not included a legend

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:41.068
is that it refers to in the title,

00:07:41.068 --> 00:07:43.620
that the recommendation on this page

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.570
is to focus on these two regions.

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:48.360
So it's kind of logical within
the structure of the page

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.520
why these two countries are highlighted.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.120
But of course you could
highlight that here as well.

00:07:56.205 --> 00:07:58.220
And then what's always helpful
with these kinds of things

00:07:58.220 --> 00:07:59.810
is to include the source.

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:02.410
So where is the data actually coming from?

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:04.420
And often it's some kind of report,

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.680
but even if you got the data
from within the company,

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.180
for instance you went to

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.900
the management accounting department,

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:14.830
and those guys gave you some data sets,

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.810
then mention the department
from where the data is from.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:20.000
This also really helps
to make it transparent.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.860
And often you will have
the discussion in a company

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.860
that people are looking
into different data sources.

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:30.310
and one department, the
data looks like this,

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:33.840
the other department the similar
data looks a bit different,

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.020
so always make sure
where it's coming from,

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.123
so that it's relevant.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:40.190
And then just the general point,

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:43.150
any other relevant
qualification of the chart,

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.852
anything else that is
necessary and is required

00:08:45.852 --> 00:08:48.270
to really understand what you're seeing,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:49.150
put it on the chart.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.300
Because people otherwise might not be able

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.320
to understand it any more later.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.070
And just one thing that comes to mind,

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:01.070
what is often done wrongly
in customer surveys

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.290
So imagine you conducted
a customer survey,

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:06.171
or you are citing some kind of results

00:09:06.171 --> 00:09:08.590
on some kind of customer surveys

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:10.725
that somebody else conducted,

00:09:10.725 --> 00:09:13.930
and what's really crucial
with this kind of data

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:18.340
is to include where the
customer survey took place.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.360
So was it a European survey?

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.600
Or was it a German survey? (chuckling)

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.370
As you probably by now
know from my accent,

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.390
I am actually coming from Germany.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.630
Or was it an American survey?

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.780
Or was it a global survey?

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:35.820
Because the implication of
the data is very different

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:38.250
if it's a global survey versus a survey

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:41.660
that was conducted in a
very specific country.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:45.340
And even in consulting
companies like mine,

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:48.771
you would find it
amazing how often you see

00:09:48.771 --> 00:09:52.550
customer survey data being sent around

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:53.851
with no clear indication,

00:09:53.851 --> 00:09:56.580
even though this of course bad practice,

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.150
people forget to write it.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:00.610
And then it's useless
because you don't really know

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:01.930
where the data is coming from,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:05.948
where it was collected, and
this really really limits

00:10:05.948 --> 00:10:08.323
the things that you can do with it.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.080
And just one small note that
I actually just catch myself,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.080
so here for the operating margin,

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:16.630
it did not have a year information.

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:20.070
So it would be good practice
to include a year as well.

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:23.773
So which year did you take
the operating margin from?

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:28.320
Okay, I hope that's kind of clear,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.470
and I hope that wasn't
too detailed for you.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:33.440
But I think it's really really
important to get that across

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.270
that you just need to make sure

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:38.570
that you put all the
information on your chart

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:40.710
that is required to understand it,

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.030
and to let it speak for itself.

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.210
Don't let other people
guess what your chart means,

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:46.690
really make it clear for them,

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:48.910
because otherwise people will be confused,

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:51.090
and your analysis won't be so helpful.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.820
Thanks again everybody for watching.

